# Prettier formats code here, not prose and not machine output.

node_modules/
dist/
build/

# Expo-generated, gitignored, and self-labelled "should not be edited". Prettier
# does not read .gitignore, so without these the format check trips on a file no
# human wrote and cannot fix by hand.
.expo/
**/expo-env.d.ts

# Markdown is hand-authored and hand-wrapped throughout this repo, with compact
# `|---|---|` tables and `*emphasis*`. Prettier rewrites both on sight, which
# would turn every docs edit into a reformatting diff and bury the actual
# change.
*.md

# NOTE — generated code is deliberately NOT ignored here.
#
# `packages/insolvia_tokens/src/tokens.ts` is generated, and it is still held to
# Prettier and ESLint like any other source: the generator is expected to emit
# correctly-formatted bytes, and `npm run tokens:check` fails the PR if a
# hand-edit ever drifts from the source of truth.
#
# The trade is real and worth knowing: a Prettier version bump that changes a
# default can fail CI on a file no human wrote, and the fix is editing the
# generator's templates — never reformatting the output, which the drift check
# would immediately reject.

# The token source of truth. Its formatting is reviewed by humans and read by
# the generator; leave it exactly as committed.
packages/insolvia_tokens/tokens.json

# Generated theme.css — the generator owns its bytes, and `npm run
# tokens:check` fails on any drift. Prettier would strip a blank line the
# generator deliberately emits, so a reformat trips that check; edit
# tokens.json and regenerate instead. This is the tokens.ts trade-off resolved
# the other way: tokens.ts bytes are cheap, theme.css bytes are expensive (see
# packages/insolvia_tokens/CLAUDE.md).
packages/insolvia_design_system/src/styles/theme.css
